title: Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm - Literature Search on UpToDate
---

# Clinical Features and diagnosis of abdominal aortic aneurysm

- **Definition** - segmental, full-thickness dilatation of a blood vessel \> 50% greater than normal diamater (normal infra-renal aorta ~ 2cm)
- **Site of abdominal aortic aneurysms:**
  - <u>Infrarenal</u> - aneurysm originates below the renal arteries
  - <u>Juxtarenal</u> - aneurysm originates at the level of renal artery, but aorta at level of renal arteries is normal
  - <u>Pararenal</u> - aneurysm originates at the level of renal artery, and renal arteries arise from an aneurysmal aorta
  - <u>Suprarenal</u> - aneurysm originates above the level of the renal arteries
- **Risk factors for AAA:**
  - <u>Modifiable risk factors</u> - e.g. HTN, hyperlipidaemia, atherosclerosis, smoking (**DM is protective against AAA**)
  - <u>Non-modifiable risk factors</u>:
    - **Demographic** - advancing age, male sex
    - **PMH:**
      - Connective tissue disorders (e.g. Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos, Loeys-Dietz syndrome)
      - Other arterial aneurysms (e.g. iliac, femoral, popliteal, intracranial)
      - Prior aortic dissection
      - Prior aortic surgery or instrumentation
    - **FHx** - +ve FHx for abdominal aortic aneurysm
- **Risk factors of aneurysm ruptures:**
  - <u>Aneurysm factor</u> - large initial aneurysm diameter (\> 5.5 cm), greater aortic expansion rate (\> 0.5cm/y)
  - <u>Patient factor</u> - female sex, high BP, current smoking
  - <u>CLinical presentation</u> - symptomatic AAA indicates impending rupture
- **Clinical presentation of AAA** - most patient are asymptomatic, whereas symptomatic patients are suggestive of rupture or impending rupture:
  - **Asymptomatic AAA** - discovered as a result of 1) screening (mainly in USA), 2) incidental finding on P/E, 3) Incidental finding on radiography
  - **Symptomatic non-ruptured AAA** (5-22% of patient) - increased risk of aneurysm ruptures:
    - **Abdominal pain** - indolent onset of abdominal pain due to compression of adjacent structures:
      - <u>Site</u> - periumbilical, but back, flank, pelvic or groin pain is also described
      - <u>Onset</u> - indolent onset (cf sudden onset in ruptured AAA)
      - <u>Quality</u> - vague, poorly localised non-specific pain
      - <u>Exacerbating and relieving factors</u> - unaffected by posture and movement
    - **Limb ischaemia** - acute limb ischaemia from distal thromboembolism of atherothrombotic debris
    - **Constitutional symptoms** - chronic abdominal pain, weight loss, low-grade fevr due to aortitis
  - **Ruptured AAA** - classical triad of abdominal pain, hypotension and pulsatile mass (25-50%)

# Management of asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm

# Management of symptomatic, non-ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm

- **Clinical presentation of symptomatic, non-ruptured AAA:**
  - <u>Abdominal pain</u> - typically periumbilical pain +/- radiation to the back, flanks, or groin
  - <u>Limb ischaemia</u> - features of acute limb ischaemia due to thromboembolism from aneurysmal atherosclerotic plaques
- **Principles of Mx** - prevention of ruptured AAA:
  - **Urgent or emergency AAA repair** - indicated for all patients with symptomatic (non-ruptured AAA) unless risk of procedure is prohibitive
  - **Choice of AAA repair** - open surgery vs EVAR dependent on:
    - 1\. Patient factor - e.g. patient comorbidities (e.g. advanced age \> 80y, ischaemic heart disease)
    - 2\. Aneurysmal factors - anatomical suitability for EVAR

